course: dictionary
lesson: 4281
title: Greedy: wanting more than your share
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Greedy means wanting much more food, money or things than you need, and not wanting to share them. Context gives you clues. A vet's example says a greedy dog swallowed a sock. The dog wanted far more than it needed, and it took something it should never eat, so greedy fits.
explain: Read the words around it to find the tone. «Greedy beggars!» is said by the Otter, who is shaking himself and grumbling in a friendly way. The words around it show it is a playful tease, not a real insult. Always check: does your meaning fit the sentence?
explain: Greedy has a second meaning in maths and computing. A greedy method picks the biggest or best choice at each step, right now. Giving change with the biggest coins first is a greedy method.
fact: Greedy is an adjective: greedy, greedier, greediest.
fact: «Greedy» means wanting much more food, money or things than you need, and not wanting to share.
fact: In stories, greedy characters often end badly, which teaches us a lesson about sharing.
fact: A greedy method in computing always picks the best choice right now, such as giving change with the biggest coins first.
fact: «Greedy beggars!» is a friendly tease between friends, not a real insult.
exercise: The Otter grumbles «Greedy beggars!» What does he mean? | A rude insult that ends the friendship | He is saying he is very cold | *A friendly tease, not a real insult | A new kind of food
exercise: A vet says a greedy dog swallowed a sock. Why is greedy a good word here? | It did not eat for a whole day | It shared the sock with the other dogs | *It wanted far more than food, so it swallowed something it should never eat | It was too tired to eat
exercise: Which describes a greedy method in computing? | Taking a random choice each time | Stopping at the first step | *Taking the biggest or best choice at each step | Sharing every choice with a friend
test: What does greedy mean? | *Wanting much more food, money or things than you need, and not wanting to share | Shining brightly in the dark | Moving quickly across a field | Being very tall and strong
test: In a story, what usually happens to greedy characters? | They always win the race | They become famous kings | *They often end badly, which teaches a lesson about sharing | They share everything and are praised
exam: Which sentence uses greedy in the same sense as "Don't be greedy, leave some for others"? | *"He grabbed all the sweets and would not share a single one" | "He gave half his lunch to his friend" | "She read her book quietly by the window" | "The river flowed softly past the old mill"
